# Data — a published number is only reproducible if the bytes it came from are
# here. Raw snapshots are immutable and small enough to track (compressed JSON);
# the SQLite store is a build product rebuilt from them, so it is not tracked.
data/raw/*
!data/raw/*/
!data/raw/**/*.json.gz
!data/raw/**/manifest.json
data/interim/
data/processed/

# Secrets — STRATZ / OpenDota keys, market API credentials
.env
*.key
config/secrets.yaml

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/

# CLI output — scratch runs are not tracked. A run bundle under reports/runs/
# is: it carries a manifest binding every output hash to the source revision,
# the input snapshot and the configs, so it is evidence rather than output.
reports/*
!reports/runs/
!reports/runs/**
# The matched-window backtest is a one-off registered diagnostic run outside
# cli_release, so it has no run bundle. Its outputs are tracked because the
# closing report quotes them and a reader should be able to check the numbers
# without a 20-minute re-run; the exact command that produced them is in
# docs/ti26/2026-08-07-d4-matched-window-spec.md.
!reports/d4_matched_window/
!reports/d4_matched_window/**

# Group card postmortem, registered in
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-16-group-card-postmortem.md. Tracked for the
# same reason as the matched-window backtest: it carries the only computed
# E_model[S] the shipped card has, and a reader should be able to check it
# without a 90-second re-run of 250000 simulations.
!reports/card_postmortem.md

# Agent harness local state — machine-local config, plugin caches, and skill
# scratch workspaces. None of it is project source.
.playwright-mcp/
.superpowers/
.claude/
.claude-plugin/
.codex/
.mcp.json
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md

# Working notes — kept locally, not published
gpt-pro-output.md

# macOS
.DS_Store
# The corrected-engine re-run of the matched-window backtest. Same
# argument list as reports/d4_matched_window/, different rules engine; both
# are published side by side in the closing report.
!reports/d4_matched_window_corrected/
!reports/d4_matched_window_corrected/**

# Series-level scoring on TI 2025, registered in
# docs/ti26/2026-08-08-ti2025-series-scoring-spec.md. Tracked for the same
# reason as the matched-window backtest: the closing report quotes it.
!reports/series_score/
!reports/series_score/**

# Corpus composition — tier mix, patch mix, recency, per-team volume and RD.
# Tracked for the same reason again: docs/ti26/2026-08-08-known-weaknesses.md
# quotes these numbers throughout, and its whole point is that a reader can
# check them rather than trust the prose.
!reports/data_health/
!reports/data_health/**

# Expert cards published before TI 2025, scored against the frozen truth. The
# external calibration in the weaknesses document rests on these two numbers,
# and an outside benchmark is worth nothing if a reader cannot re-derive it.
!reports/external_cards/
!reports/external_cards/**

# The elimination choice policy priced across all three candidates at 250k sims.
# TI 2026's rules leave the basis of that choice unspecified, so the decision to
# ship `rational` rests entirely on this measurement showing it changes no card
# slot. Tracked so the decision can be checked rather than taken on trust.
!reports/schedule_sensitivity_2026/
!reports/schedule_sensitivity_2026/**

# The bracket-rule check against TI 2025. Tracked because the provenance
# comment on `base_pairing_preference` in config/ti2026_rules.yaml now cites
# this file instead of restating a count -- the count it used to restate went
# stale twice without anyone noticing, which is the whole argument for the rule
# that a number must come from the code that emits it.
!reports/pairing_check/
!reports/pairing_check/**

# The same calendar window counted across two snapshots taken five days apart.
# It is what settles whether the thin recent tail is a real pre-major slowdown
# or an incomplete ingest, and the runbook's instruction not to delay the
# lock-day snapshot rests on it, so the numbers have to be checkable.
!reports/snapshot_lag/
!reports/snapshot_lag/**

# The card regenerated on the corrected TI 2026 rules, kept alongside the
# pre-correction bundle it supersedes so the two-slot difference -- and the fact
# that the corrected card equals the naive strength sort -- can be diffed.
!reports/card_ti2026_rules/
!reports/card_ti2026_rules/**
